window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][89] = "<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;A feature factory is a team that is rewarded for shipping features, not for what the features change. It happens because output is visible and outcomes are slow. I'd look for two signals: the share of roadmap items that have an outcome metric and target (if under half, it's a warning), and the share of shipped items that were measured afterward. If 6 of 20 items have metrics and 3 of 14 shipped items were reviewed, the team cannot tell which launches mattered.</p>\n<p>&quot;To avoid it: every item gets a metric, a baseline, a target and a review date before work begins; I keep an outcomes log; I report outcome coverage and on-time reviews beside velocity; and I stop items whose metric doesn't move after review. The check is a number: in the next quarter I'd want near 100% coverage on new items and over 80% of launches reviewed on time.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The definition; measurable signals; practices with a check.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if stakeholders want a feature without a metric? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what outcome it serves and propose a light metric and a short review date. If it truly has no outcome, I'd label it a bet, time-box it and say so.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Isn't shipping fast valuable? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Yes, when it is fast learning. The anti-pattern is shipping without checking what happened. A fast team with reviews is the goal.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-02 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is a vanity metric? Give an example and a better alternative.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A metric that looks good but means nothing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> One that rises without informing decisions, with an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives a test (would a decision change if it fell?), an example with the better metric and a guardrail, and notes the role of distributions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;A vanity metric is a number that rises reliably and flatters but does not tell you whether the product is working or what to do. My test is: if this number fell, would any decision change? Registered users is the standard example: it only grows. A better metric is weekly active users who return in each of the last four weeks, by cohort, which shows whether people keep coming back. I'd pair it with a guardrail such as complaints or cancellations so that it cannot be raised by tricks.</p>\n<p>&quot;Averages can also be vanity: a mean session length of 33 seconds can hide that 90% of sessions are 3 seconds. So I'd report the median and a share-above-threshold, such as the share of users with three meaningful sessions a week.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A decision test; a better metric with a guardrail; distributions.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is registered users always useless? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;No: it is the top of a funnel and useful for capacity or cost planning. It becomes a vanity metric when it is the goal.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know your replacement isn't vanity too? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Apply the same test: does a fall change what we do, and can it be raised without raising value? If it can be gamed, add a guardrail or a second metric.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-03 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A senior stakeholder insists you build a feature because a competitor has it. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it to stay competitive,&quot; or &quot;refuse.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the user and the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates strategy by analogy from a real gap, checks who is leaving and why, sizes the gap, offers a test, and uses a decision rule while respecting the decider.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd take the concern seriously and ask for the evidence. 'They have it' is strategy by analogy; the questions are whether customers are leaving because we lack it, how many, and what they say. I'd check churn reasons, lost-deal notes and usage of the competitor's feature if public, and ask who would use it and what job it does for them. I'd also ask how the competitor's situation differs from ours, and who has tried it and failed.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then propose a proportional step: a smoke test or a manual version, with a threshold agreed in advance, and a decision date. If the evidence shows a real gap, build it; if not, document why and keep watching. If the stakeholder decides to build it anyway and it's within their authority, I'd commit, write down my concern and the signal that would make us revisit, and measure the result.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence over analogy; a test with a rule; commitment after the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if they say there's no time to test? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd offer the cheapest evidence available that day: a look at lost-deal reasons and a few customer calls. The cost of a week of checking is small against months of building the wrong thing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the feature works for the competitor? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd learn why: their users, their positioning, their data. I'd borrow the principle, not the feature, and test it for our segment.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-04 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Users keep asking for a feature. Do you build it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, users know what they want.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the underlying need.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates requests from situations, counts who is asking and what share they represent, checks behavior, considers alternative solutions, and tests before building.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd treat the request as a symptom of a job, not as a specification. I'd ask: what are they trying to do when they want it, what do they do today, and how often? I'd count how many are asking and what share of users and revenue they are, because a loud ten may not represent a quiet thousand. I'd check behavior: do those users already work around the problem, and where do they drop?</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd list other ways to meet the need (a setting, a default, a different feature) and test the cheapest one: a fake door to see how many click, or a manual version for five customers. I build the request as asked only if it turns out to be the best way to serve the need and the numbers justify it.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Request versus job; counting and representation; alternatives; a cheap test.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the requesters are your biggest customers? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Their needs matter, and I'd check whether the need generalizes. If it is specific, a configuration or a priced custom solution may be better than a core feature. I'd say so with the numbers and offer a path to yes.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the test shows few people click? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then demand is lower than the requests suggested, and I'd tell the requesters what I learned and what I can do instead. I'd record the result so the next request is judged against it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-05 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your launch hit its date, but the metric did not move. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Wait and see,&quot; or &quot;ship another feature.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks adoption and measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses along the funnel (awareness, activation, value), checks the metric and holdout design, reads effects by week and segment, decides to iterate, stop or extend, and records the learning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First check that the measurement is sound: is the metric computed correctly, is there a holdout, and is the sample large enough? Then diagnose along the path: did users find the feature (awareness), try it (activation), and get value (repeat use)? If adoption is 4% against a 15% goal, the problem is awareness or activation, with different fixes: placement and messaging, or a simpler first use. If adoption is fine and the metric did not move, the feature may not affect the outcome, and the assumption linking them was wrong.</p>\n<p>&quot;Read by week and segment: a novelty bump that faded, or an effect in one segment that is diluted in the average. Decide by the criteria set before launch: iterate if there is a fixable path, stop if the assumption failed (and free the capacity), extend the test if it is underpowered. Then record the learning in the outcomes log.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Measurement check; funnel diagnosis; segments and weeks; a decision rule; a record.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you had no holdout? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd use before-and-after with caution, compare with similar periods, and look for a natural comparison group. I'd also add a holdout for the next launch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How long do you wait? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;As long as the review date I set: long enough for the effect to settle and a full weekly cycle, usually several weeks; I'd not extend indefinitely without a reason.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-06 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you spot your own recurring mistakes?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I reflect after projects.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Reviews artifacts and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Runs a scan on artifacts with detection signals, counts patterns, finds the root-cause cluster, chooses one practice with a retest, and uses outside examples.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I scan evidence, not memory. I take my last three specs, my roadmap with its outcome measures, my mock or review scorecards and my stories, and check each against a list of detection signals: for example, does each roadmap item have a metric, did I state the guardrail, did the story show a decision. I count how often each pattern appears, group them by root cause (activity mistaken for progress, optimizing the measure, avoiding discomfort, pattern-matching, not closing the loop), and look for the dominant cluster.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then one practice, not five: say, 'I name the guardrail in the same sentence as the metric.' I apply it to the next artifacts and recheck in three or four weeks. I also ask a teammate for one example of each pattern they've seen me do, since I can't see all of them. Last time my scan showed that in 6 of 8 mocks I gave no guardrail; after the practice and ten drills, it was 1 in 4.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence; counts; a cluster; one practice and a retest; outside views.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the scan finds many patterns? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd pick the cluster they share and one practice for it. Fixing the root often removes several symptoms.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid defensiveness when a colleague points one out? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I ask for a specific example and the effect on them, thank them, and add it to the scan. A pattern named by someone else is data I could not collect alone.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-07 · Hard · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A product has 40 million registered users, but most sessions are very short. Is it healthy?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;40 million users is great.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions the registered number and asks about engagement.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes how mean and median differ, defines meaningful use, asks how sessions arise (intent or incidental), reads cohorts and retention, and sets decision criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Not necessarily. Registered users counts everyone who ever signed up and says nothing about use. If 90% of sessions last about 3 seconds and 10% last about 300, the mean is 32.7 seconds, which looks fine, but the median is 3 seconds and 92% of the time comes from 10% of sessions. So I'd measure the median and the share of users with repeated meaningful sessions, say three sessions of at least 30 seconds a week, and retention by cohort.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd ask how short sessions arise: from users who intend to be there, or from links, notifications or integrations in other products that land them there? Incidental sessions are not interest. I'd also check who the long sessions are: a loyal minority is a possible niche; no one is a problem. Decision criteria: if fewer than, say, a tenth of registered users meet the engagement bar and cohort retention falls to near zero, the issue is not growth but a missing reason to return, and I'd narrow to the segment with real use or stop.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Distribution; meaningful use; origin of sessions; cohorts; a decision rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the company has a strategic reason to keep the product? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then it should be explicit and bounded: say what the product is for (for example a shared identity service), measure that, and stop treating consumer engagement as the goal. An unstated strategic reason hides failing measures.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you decide when to stop? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With kill criteria set in advance on engagement and retention, reviewed on a date, with a narrower alternative considered first. Stopping is cheaper when it is planned.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c29-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C29-08 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You discover your team has been optimizing a metric that does not matter for the last six months. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Switch metrics and move on.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tells the team and leadership and resets the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Verifies the finding, owns your part, quantifies what was lost, tells stakeholders early with a plan, reconstructs which work still matters, sets the right metric with a guardrail and a review date, and changes the practice that allowed it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First verify: what exactly doesn't matter, and what is the evidence (no relation to retention or revenue in cohorts, say). Then tell my manager and the team promptly, in person and then in writing: here is what we found, what it cost (say, six months of two-thirds of our effort), what I think we should do and when I'll update. I'd own my part, since I helped set the goal, without self-flagellation.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then reset: choose the metric that tracks value (a retained-user measure, with a guardrail), set a baseline and a target, and review the past six months of work against it to see what still helps: some of it likely does. Decide what to stop, keep and reshape, with numbers. And I'd change the practice: every goal metric gets a documented link to an outcome, checked quarterly against cohorts, so this can't recur silently.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Verification; early disclosure with a plan; ownership; salvage; a changed practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell leadership without damaging trust? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Early, factual and with a plan. Leaders react worse to learning it from someone else. I'd say what we know, what we don't, and the decision I need from them.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the team resists changing? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd show the evidence and the cohort chart, involve them in choosing the new metric, and recognize the work that remains valuable. People accept a reset more easily when the learning is credited to the team.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c29-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-01 · Classify the scenarios</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each scenario, name the anti-pattern, the cluster (A to E), the detection signal and a one-line fix: (a) the team celebrates 14 launches and cannot say what changed; (b) the PM tests a new idea only with friends and colleagues; (c) a PM sends a 500-word weekly update listing meetings; (d) a stakeholder sets the roadmap in a meeting and no one challenges it; (e) a candidate names a framework and applies it to every prompt; (f) a team reports registered users as its north star.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Using the catalog.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C29.2 to C29.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Feature factory; A/E; low outcome coverage and shipped-and-measured share; metrics and review dates on every item. (b) Validating with friends; D/B; sample is known contacts; recruit from the target segment, including lapsed users. (c) Status as activity; A; no reader can state the status or the ask; headline, change, next, need. (d) HiPPO decision; C; no agreed evidence rule; a decision rule and disagree-and-commit. (e) Framework recitation; D; the answer would fit any prompt; reason from the user and the decision. (f) Vanity metric as goal; B; the metric rises while retention is flat; a value-linked metric with a guardrail.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a second possible pattern for each and say which evidence would decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> One scenario can show several patterns; pick the one the evidence supports.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-02 · Roadmap ratios</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A roadmap has 30 items; 9 have an outcome metric; 22 have shipped; 5 of the shipped items were reviewed after launch. Compute outcome coverage, the share of shipped items reviewed and the share of the roadmap with a closed loop. What do you conclude and do?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Feature-factory scan.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Outcome coverage: 9 ÷ 30 = 30%. Shipped items reviewed: 5 ÷ 22 = 23%. Closed loop across the roadmap: 5 ÷ 30 = 17%. The team is output-oriented (A) and does not close loops (E). Fix: require a metric, baseline, target and review date on new items; run quick reviews of the 17 unreviewed shipped items; report coverage and on-time reviews. Retest in six weeks: at least 90% coverage on new items and 80% on-time reviews.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Set the threshold by risk: items touching the core flow must have metrics; low-risk fixes may not.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Metrics for every small change create overhead; scale them to the stakes.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-03 · Mean and median</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In a day, 800,000 sessions last about 4 seconds and 200,000 last about 120 seconds. Compute the mean session length, the median and the share of total session time in the long sessions. What would you report instead of the mean?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Distributions behind a headline number.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Mean: (800,000 × 4 + 200,000 × 120) ÷ 1,000,000 = 27.2 seconds. Median: 4 seconds (80% of sessions are short). Long sessions' share of time: 24,000,000 ÷ 27,200,000 = 88%. Report the median, the 90th percentile, the share of users with at least three meaningful sessions a week and weekly retention by cohort. Ask how the short sessions arise.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Report the distribution as a histogram with buckets of seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> One number is easy to track and hides the shape; two or three show it.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-04 · Capacity and promises</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team of 4 engineers has a 10-week cycle. About 25% of time goes to unplanned work. The roadmap commits dates to items estimated at 66 engineer-weeks. Compute effective capacity and the commitment ratio; propose a commitment policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Roadmap as a promise.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Gross capacity 4 × 10 = 40 engineer-weeks; effective 40 × 0.75 = 30. Commitment ratio 66 ÷ 30 = 220%. Policy: commit to outcomes and ranges, not dated features; commit to about 70% of effective capacity (21 engineer-weeks) and hold the rest as buffer; require a published tradeoff for any addition; keep a dated promise only where there is a legal or contractual deadline. Retest: the share of committed items delivered, and the number of late date changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Express the plan as &quot;now, next, later&quot; with confidence levels (high, medium, low) and update monthly.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fewer promises give less certainty to sales and more reliability to the team; agree the cost with stakeholders.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-05 · Scan your own artifacts</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Run the anti-pattern scan (FW-C29-01) on three of your own artifacts (for example a spec, an update and your last two mock scorecards). Mark signals, count patterns, find the dominant cluster, choose one practice and set a retest date.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Your artifacts.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Sixty minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C29-01 and FW-C29-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A good scan has a table of artifacts against signals, counts by pattern (for instance, &quot;no outcome metric: 2 of 3 documents&quot;; &quot;no guardrail: 2 of 2 mocks&quot;), a dominant cluster named with evidence, one practice with the artifact where it will appear, and a retest date with the signal to recheck. The practice is specific: &quot;name the guardrail in the same sentence as the metric,&quot; not &quot;be more rigorous.&quot; Share it with a colleague and ask them for one example of each pattern they have seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Scan a teammate's roadmap with their permission as practice, using the catalog on evidence, and report signals, not labels.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A scan of three artifacts is quick and small; repeat it every quarter.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-06 · A failure story in six parts</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Using FW-C29-03, write a three-minute failure story for this scenario: you launched a notifications redesign that raised opens by 8% and opt-outs doubled; you reported the opens as success for six weeks before noticing the opt-outs.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Interview use of the catalog.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent two numbers and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ten minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C29-03 and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>What happened:</em> &quot;I launched a notification redesign that raised opens by 8% and I reported it as a success; six weeks later I saw that daily opt-outs had risen from 0.6% to 1.2% (invented), which meant we were losing the ability to reach about 7,000 users a month (invented).&quot; <em>Pattern:</em> optimizing the measure (cluster B): a single metric with no guardrail. <em>Root cause in my control:</em> I defined success as opens and never wrote a counter-metric. <em>Signal I missed:</em> opt-outs were in the dashboard from day one; I had not looked. <em>Practice changed:</em> every metric card now names a guardrail and I check both weekly; for notifications, opt-outs and unsubscribes are a launch gate. <em>Evidence:</em> in the next two notification changes we caught a rise in opt-outs in week one and rolled one back, avoiding about 4,000 lost reach (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Tell it as a post-mortem in writing, with the same six parts.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A precise failure story takes preparation and gives credibility.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-07 · Pattern or deliberate choice?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each decision say whether it is an anti-pattern or a defensible choice in context, and what evidence would decide: (a) a team launches to all users at once the week a law takes effect; (b) a PM builds a small internal tool before testing demand, because a test would take longer than the build; (c) a PM waits for a fix before telling leadership about a data error that affects a board report due in two days; (d) a team ships a feature with no outcome metric because it is a legal requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Applying the catalog with judgment.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C29.1 (when not to use the catalog).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Defensible if the date is fixed by law and the scope is limited to what the law requires; the staged rollout is replaced by a very short, heavily monitored release with a rollback plan; evidence: the regulation's date and the cost of missing it. (b) Defensible when the cost of a build is below the cost of a test and the tool is reversible; still write the success measure. (c) An anti-pattern: bad news held back; tell leadership today with the impact and the plan, since the board report is the reason to tell, not to wait. (d) Defensible: a legal requirement needs a compliance measure (done, audited), not an outcome metric; record it as such. The test: what would the pattern's fix cost here, and what would it protect against?</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Write the condition under which each defensible choice would become an anti-pattern (for example, (b) if the tool is later used by customers).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Judging context takes more thought than applying a rule and avoids false alarms.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c29-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C29-08 · Five whys to a cause you can change</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> The event: &quot;the launch slipped three weeks.&quot; Write a chain of five whys that ends in a cause within your control, and the practice that would address it. Invent plausible links and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Root cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The five-whys model in section 9.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) Why did it slip? An integration failed late testing (invented). (2) Why late? Testing started two weeks before launch. (3) Why so late? The plan scheduled testing after the last feature was complete. (4) Why? We had no dependency list or early test of the riskiest integration. (5) Why?</p>\n<p>I did not ask engineering for the riskiest technical assumption when I wrote the plan. The cause in my control is the last one. Practice: list the riskiest technical assumptions with engineering at kickoff and schedule a test of the top one in the first third of the project. Check: the share of projects where the top-risk test happened in the first third, and the number of late-found issues.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Stop at the third why if the cause there is already something you can change, and say why.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Five questions can drift into blame; end at a choice you made, not at a person.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: the catalog and the scan.</em> Use IQ-C29-01, IQ-C29-02, IQ-C29-06, EX-C29-01, EX-C29-02 and EX-C29-05. Scan three of your own artifacts and write your top pattern, its cluster and one practice.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: failure stories and diagnosis.</em> Use IQ-C29-03, IQ-C29-04, IQ-C29-05, IQ-C29-07, IQ-C29-08, EX-C29-03, EX-C29-04 and EX-C29-06. Write and rehearse a three-minute failure story with a named pattern and numbers, and practice the &quot;why did this product fail&quot; diagnosis aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;Isn't that just bad luck?&quot; and &quot;How do you know the fix works?&quot; at random.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I name the pattern, not just the event?</li>\n<li>Is my root cause a choice I made?</li>\n<li>Did I name a measurable signal that was available?</li>\n<li>Is my fix a practice with an artifact and a check?</li>\n<li>Did I give a before-and-after number?</li>\n<li>Did I scan across artifacts, not one data point?</li>\n<li>Did I avoid labeling other people's decisions?</li>\n<li>Did I ask whether context made the pattern deliberate?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the prompt, the pattern, the signal, the practice and the number, your score and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter to your own work over three weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Collect three artifacts (a spec or proposal, a status update, a roadmap or backlog with outcome measures) and the last two mock scorecards if you have them.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Run the scan (FW-C29-01): mark signals, count patterns, find the dominant cluster. Ask one teammate for one example of each pattern they have seen in your work.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Choose one practice for the dominant cluster and apply it to the next artifacts. Write your failure story (FW-C29-03) for the pattern you found, with a number.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Retest the signal on the new artifacts. If it has changed, write the before-and-after; if not, change the practice and retest again.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the scan table (evidence that you diagnose yourself), the practice and its retest result (a changed habit with a number), and the failure story (a ready answer to the commonest behavioral question). Keep every number real and credit people who helped you see the pattern.</p>\n<span id=\"c29-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c29-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Anti-patterns are common, sensible-looking responses that produce worse results; most grow from five roots: activity mistaken for progress, optimizing the measure, avoiding discomfort, pattern-matching instead of reasoning, and not closing the loop.</li>\n<li>Every anti-pattern has a detection signal you can measure: outcome coverage and shipped-and-measured share for a feature factory, a distribution behind a headline number, committed work against effective capacity, guardrail errors across mocks.</li>\n<li>Use the catalog on yourself: scan your own artifacts, count patterns, find the dominant cluster, choose one practice and retest; do not label other people's decisions.</li>\n<li>Tell a failure as a pattern with a root cause in your control, a missed signal, a changed practice and evidence it works; a fix is credible only as a habit with an artifact and a check.</li>\n<li>Check whether context made a pattern deliberate (a legal deadline can justify a big-bang launch; a costly test can justify building first) before calling it a mistake.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c29-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Google's posts of 8 October 2018 (&quot;Project Strobe&quot;) and 10 December 2018 (&quot;Expediting changes to Google+&quot;), and Google Workspace's post of 30 January 2019. Sources for CS-C29-02 (confirmed in October 2026).</li>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2017), and Melissa Perri, <em>Escaping the Build Trap</em> (2018), on output-focused teams and outcome-focused alternatives <code>[V: Cagan: 2nd edition, Wiley, December 2017; Perri: O'Reilly, November 2018]</code>.</li>\n<li>Charles Goodhart's observation on measures becoming targets, as discussed in management writing, and the references in C05 <code>[V: Goodhart 1975; Strathern 1997]</code>.</li>\n<li>Chapters C05, C06, C15, C16, C17, C26, C31, C32 and C34 of this book, which teach the fixes this chapter points to.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c30\"></span>\n<h2>C30 — Five End-to-End Product Journeys</h2>\n<span id=\"c30-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Walk me through a product you took from an idea to a launch.&quot; &quot;How would you build this from nothing, and how would you know when to stop?&quot; Interviewers rarely test a single skill in isolation; they test whether the skills fit together: whether discovery changes the spec, the spec defines what to measure, the first version tests the riskiest assumption, the kill criteria are set before the results, and the retrospective changes the next decision. This chapter shows five complete journeys, one product at a time, so you can see the earlier chapters working together. The products and every number are invented; their purpose is to demonstrate the connections and the reasoning, which you can then apply to your own work and to open-ended product questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: end-to-end product and execution questions at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, and the behavioral prompt &quot;tell me about a product you shipped&quot; <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the shape of every project.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your account of a product has a through-line <code>Inferred</code>: a problem with evidence, a riskiest assumption, a cheap test with a threshold, a spec that states non-goals and acceptance criteria, a metric tree with guardrails, a decision to continue or stop, and a lesson that changed a habit.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You connect each stage to the evidence from the stage before.</li>\n<li>You name the riskiest assumption and test it with the cheapest experiment that can fail.</li>\n<li>You write a spec with a problem statement, goals, non-goals and acceptance criteria.</li>\n<li>You build a metric tree with a primary measure, drivers and guardrails.</li>\n<li>You set kill criteria before the results arrive.</li>\n<li>You close with a retrospective that names a pattern and a changed practice.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end end-to-end answers: a list of activities with no decisions, a spec written after the build, metrics chosen after the results, no kill criteria, a launch described as the finish line, and a retrospective that is a list of virtues.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c30-c30-1-how-to-use-the-journeys\"></span>\n<h4>C30.1 How to use the journeys</h4>\n<p><strong>These are <code>Illustrative</code>, not case studies.</strong> Every product, team, interview, test result and figure in section 5 is invented (invented), built to teach how the pieces connect. They are not records of any real company or product, and the numbers are not benchmarks. A real, public lifecycle model is reviewed in CS-C30-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Read each journey three times.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>For the structure:</em> cover the results and write what you would do at each stage; compare.</li>\n<li><em>For the reasoning:</em> at each decision, find the evidence that drove it and ask what else could have been decided.</li>\n<li><em>For the numbers:</em> recompute the figures and note which assumption each rests on.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Do not memorize them.</strong> An interviewer who asks about a project of yours will probe the details (C31.7); a memorized journey collapses at the second question. Use the journeys to build the habits and the vocabulary, then tell your own story from your own work (CS-C30-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Use them as templates for open-ended questions.</strong> When asked to &quot;build a product for X,&quot; the journey template (FW-C30-01) gives a skeleton for a 30-minute answer: the user and problem, the evidence you would gather, the riskiest assumption and its test, the first version, the metric tree and the decision rule.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked to walk through a product they shipped, a candidate says: &quot;We did user research, then design, then engineering built it over three months, and we launched in June.&quot; The interviewer asks what the riskiest assumption was and what would have made the team stop. The candidate has listed activities and cannot name a decision, a threshold or a result. A better start: &quot;The riskiest assumption was that parents would pay, so before building we ran a manual version for 40 families with a threshold of 10% paying and a stop below 5%; it reached 15%, so we built.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>When not to use the full template.</strong> A small, reversible change needs a paragraph and a staged rollout, not seven parts and a kill-criteria card (C17). Do not present the template as how the work actually happened if it was messier: say where you were wrong, because a tidy story invites doubt (C31.7). And do not force a journey structure onto a question that only asks for one stage; answer the stage asked.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-c30-2-five-journeys-at-a-glance\"></span>\n<h4>C30.2 Five journeys at a glance</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Journey</th>\n<th>Distinct challenge</th>\n<th>Chapters exercised</th>\n<th>Setting</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A consumer learning companion for exam preparation, with an AI tutor</td>\n<td>Trust in AI answers; low-data use; a paying parent and a using student</td>\n<td>C06, C20, C24, C26</td>\n<td>India (low-end phones, regional languages)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>An enterprise operations tool for warehouse exceptions</td>\n<td>A long sales cycle; security review; integration; roadmap commitments</td>\n<td>C16, C17, C27</td>\n<td>Global</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A two-sided marketplace with a cold start: small shops and wholesalers</td>\n<td>Supply and demand together; credit; manual start</td>\n<td>C19, C26, C27</td>\n<td>India (neighborhood retail)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>An internal AI evaluation platform</td>\n<td>Customers are other teams; adoption without a mandate; measuring a judge</td>\n<td>C21, C25, C27</td>\n<td>Global</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>An autonomous agent for knowledge work (purchase requests)</td>\n<td>Reliability of a chain; approvals; injection; earned autonomy</td>\n<td>C24, C25, C32</td>\n<td>Global</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>At most two journeys are India-specific (1 and 3); the others are set in a general global context.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-c30-3-the-per-journey-template\"></span>\n<h4>C30.3 The per-journey template</h4>\n<p>Each journey in section 5 follows the same seven parts (FW-C30-01), so you can compare them:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Context and users.</strong> Who, what job, what alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Discovery.</strong> The evidence gathered, the riskiest assumption, the cheapest test.</li>\n<li><strong>Spec excerpt.</strong> Problem statement, goals, non-goals, key requirements, acceptance criteria (C16).</li>\n<li><strong>Metrics tree.</strong> Primary measure, drivers, guardrails (C05, C27.10).</li>\n<li><strong>MVP, kill criteria and results.</strong> The smallest test, the thresholds set in advance, the readout (C26).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The decisions with their alternatives and costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Retrospective.</strong> What went wrong, the anti-pattern (C29), the practice changed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Where each part is taught:</strong> discovery in C06 and C26; the spec in C16; metrics in C05 and C27; kill criteria in C26; rollout in C17; AI specifics in C24 and C25; domain concepts in C27; communication in C32; failure patterns in C29.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-c30-4-closing-what-the-five-journeys-teach\"></span>\n<h4>C30.4 Closing: what the five journeys teach</h4>\n<p>Reading across the journeys shows recurring structure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The riskiest assumption was different each time, and it was never the build.</strong> Willingness to pay and trust (1), the buying process and integration (2), supply behavior (3), adoption (4), reliability and trust in approvals (5). The cheapest test addressed that assumption first.</li>\n<li><strong>A manual or narrow first version beat a built one.</strong> A concierge test in 1 and 3, three pilot sites in 2, five teams in 4, a draft-only mode in 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Kill criteria were set before the data and the results were mixed.</strong> In several journeys the result passed one criterion and not another, and the decision came from the pre-set rule plus judgment about the segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-feature requirements shaped the plan.</strong> Security review (2), parental consent and data limits (1), credit and payments rules (3), permissions and cost (4), injection and audit (5).</li>\n<li><strong>Each retrospective named an anti-pattern from C29</strong> and a practice change with a check: roadmap as a promise (2), a vanity count (4), the demo as evidence (5), a test sample that was too easy (1), a supply-side target (3).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What the journeys do not show.</strong> They are tidy: real projects have false starts, politics and changes of mind. The value is in the connections between stages, which you should be able to reproduce with your own, messier, evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Three frameworks, set out below. The problem-definition template (C02.7), the PRD template (C16), the metric card (FW-C05-01), the riskiest-assumption map and test card (FW-C26-04, FW-C26-05), the kill-persist-pivot table (FW-C26-06), the autonomy ladder (FW-C24-05), the trust progression (FW-C25-05), the anti-pattern scan (FW-C29-01) and the failure-story structure (FW-C29-03) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c30-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C30-01 · The journey template · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product idea or a product you shipped.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a one-page account in seven parts: context and users; discovery (evidence, riskiest assumption, test); spec excerpt (problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, acceptance criteria); metrics tree with guardrails; MVP with kill criteria and results; tradeoffs; retrospective with a pattern and a practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> each part is justified by the evidence of the part before.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a template makes the through-line visible and can make a messy project look tidier than it was; say where it was not.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a spec with no connection to discovery; metrics chosen after results; no retrospective.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the five journeys in section 5.</li>\n</ul>\n";
